INT. CURLING RINK - 5 A.M.

A long sheet of pebbled ice under cold blue light. The rafters are hung with faded club banners. Frost on the glass. The Zamboni sits idle at the far end like a sleeping animal.

Four women stand at the hog line in padded jackets, brooms upright. Breath rises from them in synchronized clouds.

GRETCHEN VANDERMEER, 50s, the skip, holds the house in her gaze as if it might move if she looked away. BERNADETTE "BERNIE" OKAFOR, 40s, the lead, flexes a wrist wrapped in tape. LINNEA SOLBERG, 30s, third, adjusts a glove for the tenth time. And at the back, 19-year-old XIMENA TREVINO, the new second, holds her broom as if it might fire.

GRETCHEN
We are down by one. Last end. Hammer is theirs.

BERNIE
They always have the hammer.

LINNEA
They always have the luck.

GRETCHEN
Luck is a story we tell after.

XIMENA
Should I sweep hard or light?

GRETCHEN
Hard until I say light. Light until I say hard.

XIMENA
That's the same thing.

GRETCHEN
Then you understand perfectly.

LINNEA
She will, in a year.

BERNIE
In a year she will be skipping.

XIMENA
I don't want to skip.

GRETCHEN
No one wants to skip. We want the rock to go where we want it. The skip is the one who stands there when it doesn't.

BERNIE
That's the most poetic thing she's said all season.

LINNEA
It was a little bit poetic.

GRETCHEN
It was a rule. Eyes on the broom.

XIMENA
Okay. Eyes on the broom.

GRETCHEN
Bernie, first stone. Draw to the four-foot. Light touch.

BERNIE
Light as a prayer.

GRETCHEN
Do not say "prayer."

BERNIE
Light as an apology.

GRETCHEN
Better.

Bernie settles into the hack. The ice hisses under her slider as she pushes out, long and low. She releases the stone, and it glides away with a low whisper, the handle turning slowly.

GRETCHEN (CONT'D)
(shouting)
Sweep!

Linnea and Ximena drop and sweep, brooms flicking in perfect alternating rhythm. Their breath comes in hard bursts. The stone drifts, curls, slows.

GRETCHEN (CONT'D)
Hold. Hold. Off.

They lift their brooms. The stone slides to a stop, dead center of the house. A tiny sound, like a coin settling on a table.

Nobody cheers. They only look at it, steaming faintly on the ice.
